            "Twist: Helix Angle and Yarn Strength",
            "Twist binds staple fibres into a coherent yarn, and its geometry - the surface helix angle - explains why strength rises with twist to an optimum and then falls away.",
            _f("Twist (helix) angle", r"\tan\alpha=\pi\,d\,T,\qquad T=\text{turns per unit length}",
               (("alpha", "surface fibre helix angle", "degrees"), ("d", "yarn diameter", "m"),
                ("T", "twist level", "turns/m")),
               "Ideal helical fibre migration; strength-optimum angles are ~20-35 degrees for cotton-type staples, and fibre length, fineness and friction shift the optimum."),
            "Without twist, a staple yarn is a bundle that pulls apart fibre by fibre - strength is only friction. Inserting twist makes surface fibres helices: when the yarn is tensioned, each helix wants to straighten, converting axial load into radial compression that grips its neighbours - cohesion by geometry. More twist raises that compression, so strength climbs; but the same helix angle increasingly misaligns fibres with the load axis, wasting their tenacity on oblique pulling, and past the optimum angle the misalignment wins and strength falls. The tan(alpha) = pi d T relation lets a spinner convert any twist-per-metre spec into the angle where that trade-off lives.",
            _e("A yarn of 0.20 mm diameter carries 800 turns per metre. Find the surface helix angle and assess it against the strength optimum.",
               "Apply the twist-angle relation.",
               "tan(alpha) = pi x 2e-4 x 800 = 0.503, so alpha = arctan(0.503) = 26.7 degrees.",
               "About 27 degrees - squarely inside the 20-35 degree strength-optimum band for cotton-type staples; adding twist toward 1200 tpm (alpha ~ 37 degrees) would push past the optimum into declining strength and a harder, harsher hand.",),
            ("Spinning frames set twist per count and fibre to hit strength and hand targets.", "Troubleshooting yarn breaks: under-twisted yarns are weak and hairy, over-twisted are harsh and snarling - the angle locates the fault."),
            "Specifying twist per metre without diameter (the angle, not the count, is the physics), or expecting the same optimum for long-staple wool and short cotton.",
