            "Wind power scales with the cube of speed and the swept area, but physics caps the extractable share at 59.3% - the Betz limit - before any machine inefficiency is counted.",
            _f("Wind power and the Betz coefficient", r"P=\tfrac{1}{2}\rho A V^{3}C_{p},\qquad C_{p}\le \frac{16}{27}=0.593",
               (("rho", "air density (~1.225 kg/m^3 at sea level)", "kg/m^3"), ("A", "rotor swept area pi D^2/4", "m^2"),
                ("V", "wind speed at hub height", "m/s"), ("C_p", "power coefficient achieved by the rotor", "-")),
               "Actuator-disc, incompressible flow; real turbines reach C_p ~ 0.45-0.50 near rated speed, and the cubic V dependence dominates siting economics."),
            "The kinetic-energy flux through the swept disc is half rho A V cubed. A rotor that stopped the air completely would collect all of it but pass zero mass flow; one that ignores the wind collects nothing. Optimizing the trade-off - how much to slow the stream - gives a maximum extractable fraction of 16/27, the Betz limit, reached when downstream speed is one third of upstream. Real rotors then pay aerodynamic, tip-loss and drivetrain tolls, landing at C_p near 0.45-0.5. The cubic speed term is the siting story: a site averaging 8 m/s carries twice the power density of one at 6.35 m/s - a 20% speed gain doubles the energy, which is why hub heights keep climbing.",
